{"name":"Lap Pool Blue","hex":"#98bbb7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lap","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Kabba-Laka."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The loose part of a coat; the lower part of a garment that plays loosely; a skirt; an apron.","An edge; a border; a hem, as of cloth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lap"},{"term":"pool","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small and rather deep area of (usually) fresh water, as one supplied by a spring, or occurring in the course of a stream or river; a reservoir for water.","Any small body of standing or stagnant water; a puddle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form a pool."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pool"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Swimming pool","blocks":[{"text":"A swimming pool, swimming bath, wading pool, paddling pool, or simply pool, is a structure designed to hold water to enable swimming and associated activities. Pools can be built into the ground or built above ground, and may be found as a feature aboard ships. In-ground pools are most commonly constructed from materials such as concrete, natural stone, metal, plastic, composite or fiberglass, and may follow a standardized size, the largest of which is the Olympic-size swimming pool, or be of a custom shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Backyardpool.jpg/330px-Backyardpool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swimming_pool","wikidata":"Q1501"},{"title":"Lap","blocks":[{"text":"A lap is a surface created between the knee and hips of a biped when it is in a seated or lying down position. The lap of a parent or loved one is seen as a physically and psychologically comfortable place for a child to sit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/%27In_slaap_gesust%27_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-2378.jpeg/330px-%27In_slaap_gesust%27_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-2378.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lap","wikidata":"Q1276686"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Olympic_Swimming_Pool_-_Fast_Lane.JPG/500px-Olympic_Swimming_Pool_-_Fast_Lane.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Olympic_Swimming_Pool_-_Fast_Lane.JPG/500px-Olympic_Swimming_Pool_-_Fast_Lane.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu